{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Words of</b>. Some interpret the first verse thus: wisdom is better than might. Yet had the poor man's wisdom been despised, his words, though spoken in quiet and calm, would be better heard than those of a ruler shouting among fools, for a wise man's words are like driven nails, fixed in the soul. Others say: the words of a single sage are not heard until they are joined together. Others say: these sages are rich. The correct view in my eyes is that he is not speaking of one time, as I have explained; and even if the poor man's wisdom is despised, there is a time when they will hear his words more than the words of the king."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The Hebrew repeats על על ('about about') before עת אחת; rendered as if a single preposition, possibly a scribal duplication."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:46.328720654Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}